    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters June 16 with a lineup optimized for high-upside matchups against mediocre arms. Our strongest play of the day is **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.72), who benefits from a prime cleanup-adjacent spot and a favorable matchup against M. Keller. Similarly, **James Wood** (72.56) is locked in as a daily staple; his elite .407 xwOBA paired with a high-leverage leadoff spot makes him a high-floor candidate for run production. The most tactical play of the day belongs to **Brice Turang** (66.63); while his base projection is solid, the matchup against Cecconi presents a rare \"slow delivery\" overlay that creates an exploitable window for stolen bases, arguably our most critical category.\n\nWe are navigating several complex platoon dynamics today. **Seiya Suzuki** (68.63) overcomes a slight platoon disadvantage against Feltner to remain a \"Strong\" start due to his favorable batting order slot, while **Austin Riley** (64.21) remains in the lineup to leverage a weak SP_xERA of 4.71. Conversely, **Corbin Carroll** (52.12) falls into a \"Marginal\" start category. Despite his talent, his matchup against the southpaw Detmers\u2014combined with the pitcher's elite 3.19 xERA\u2014drastically suppresses his ceiling. Our most difficult decision was keeping **Alex Freeland** (37.77) on the bench. He hovered near the 40.0 floor, but Rasmussen\u2019s 3.36 xERA coupled with an unfavorable bottom-of-the-order assignment ultimately kept him out of the starting nine. We remain cautious regarding **Jose Caballero** (52.17); while his power profile is decent, his -13% platoon adjustment against Martin is a significant drag on his daily efficiency, and he remains a candidate for mid-game substitution if the game script shifts.",
